To check whether an Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) cluster is undergoing or has scheduled regular maintenance, here are a few ways to find out:

🧰 1. Check Maintenance Configuration (AKS Maintenance Window)
AKS allows configuring planned maintenance windows. You can check the maintenance configuration using the Azure CLI:

bash
Copy
Edit
az aks show --name <cluster-name> --resource-group <resource-group> --query maintenanceWindow
This will show if there is a maintenance window configured (like specific days/times when updates are allowed).

📝 2. Look for Ongoing Node Upgrades / Repairs
You can check if the cluster is currently being maintained (e.g., nodes being upgraded) using:

bash
Copy
Edit
az aks nodepool list --resource-group <resource-group> --cluster-name <cluster-name> --output table
Look for Upgrade in Progress, Updating, or changes in PowerState or ProvisioningState.

🔍 3. Use Azure Activity Log
Go to the Activity Log in the Azure portal for your AKS resource group:

Filter by “AKS” and look for operations like UpgradeCluster, UpgradeNodePool, Patch, or Reimage.

This can show if Azure performed maintenance recently.

📦 4. Check Node Kubelet Versions
Maintenance can include automatic patching or updates. Compare node versions:

bash
Copy
Edit
kubectl get nodes -o wide
Look for inconsistent VERSION fields or recent changes which could mean nodes were rotated or updated.

🛠 5. Azure Service Health Alerts
If you've set up Service Health alerts, you can check if any were triggered for planned or unplanned maintenance affecting your cluster.

